class Solution {
    public TreeNode lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {

        // Base case
        if (root == null || root == p || root == q) {
            return root;
        }

        // Search in left subtree
        TreeNode left = lowestCommonAncestor(root.left, p, q);

        // Search in right subtree
        TreeNode right = lowestCommonAncestor(root.right, p, q);

        // p and q are on different sides
        if (left != null && right != null) {
            return root;
        }

        // Return whichever side found p or q
        if (left != null) {
            return left;
        }

        return right;
    }
}

I use recursive DFS.
 At every node, I first check whether the node is null, p, or q. If it is p or q, I return that node.
 Then I recursively search the left and right subtrees.
 If both left and right return a non-null node, it means p and q were found on different sides, so the current node is their LCA.
 If only one side returns a non-null node, I return that side because both nodes must be somewhere in that subtree."
 Time:  O(n)
Space: O(h)